}}|subdivision_type1=[[Communities of Balisca|Community]]|subdivision_name1={{flag|Estuaria}}|area_km2=321571.24|population_census=3,621,980|population_census_year=2025|regional_languages=|common_languages=[[Baliscano]], [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], [[Estuarian Creole language|Estuarian Creole]]|cctld=.qsc|mapsize=280px|ethnic_groups_year=2023|ethnic_groups=72.7% [[Baliscans]]<br/> 27.3% [[Demographics of Balisca|others]]|demonym=Quiscostan <br> Quiscostenc (m), Quiscostenca (f)|population_census_rank=16th}}'''Quiscosta''' (meaning "shrimp coast"; {{lang-ec|Kostapia}}) is one of the four regions of [[Estuaria]], within the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. It is bounded to north and west by the [[Salsipuedes Sea]], the northeast by [[Mesopotàmia]], and the south by [[Potocsí]]. The region's capital and largest city is [[Aigües|Aigües dels Criolls]].

Various Indigenous groups, most prominently [[Jasyrujupi|Jasyrujupi-speaking peoples]], have inhabited this for at least 8,000 years. In 1505, Volisanian explorer [[Aquil·les Balthazar Feixó]] and his crew became the first Illypnian to explore the area, calling the entire region ''la Flórida'' for its lush flora. Quiscosta subsequently became one of the earliest areas of permanent Illypno-Mesogean settlement in the [[Lurando-Abyala]], and was repeatedly contested between Jacano, Sartan and Cathar settlers as part of the [[Reapers' War]], in which it successfully resisted the [[expulsion of the Tarracos]]. It sent delegates to the [[Articles of Confederation]], and signed the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]]. Like all the other Volisanian-speaking communities in the lead-up to the [[Baliscan Civil War]], Quiscosta opposed the secession of Bayara. It was invaded by secessionist forces in 1828 and subsequently occupied, during which it experienced widespread destruction and looting.

Much of the regions lands were formed from sediment washed down the [[Aín (river)|Aín]] (''Ahín''), [[Daimús (river)|Daimús]] (''Daimuz'') and [[Canyada (river)|Canyada]] (''Cañada'') rivers, as well as their tributaries as part of the [[Estuaria delta|Aín–Canyada–Daimús delta]]. This left vast areas of {{W|coastal marsh}} and {{W|swamp}}. It became one of the last landmasses of Abyala to emerge from the ocean. There are to vast lakes in its interior and extensive areas of coastal {{W|marshlands}}. These contain a rich {{W|biome|biota}}; typical examples include birds such as {{W|storks}}, {{W|toucans}} and {{W|egrets}}. There are also many species of {{W|tree frogs}}, and fish such as {{W|Salminus brasiliensis|golden dorado}} and {{W|salmon}}. 

